Zune68 [RELEASE] - [STAGE] for AmigaOS 3.x / m68k
===============================================

Zune68 is a user interface library based on AROS Zune, targeting
compatibility with classic MUI applications.
The full package contains the core libraries, the Zune preferences editor,
[PLUGINS] optional MUI class plugins, among them the Rawimage/Pixmap
image classes and the AROS utility classes, translation catalogs,
opentest, eight examples, SDK headers, plugin documentation/licenses,
and corresponding source.
The separate core-only packaging option omits the additional components.

Requirements
------------
AmigaOS 3.1 or newer, a 68000 or better, and Amiga Installer 43 or newer.
The system IEEE double math libraries must be available; no FPU is needed.
Installer itself is not included. The Install icon uses the default tool
"installer". If Workbench cannot find it, set the icon's Default Tool to
its full path, such as SYS:System/Installer on AmigaOS 3.2.3.

Installation
------------
Extract the archive and double-click Install inside the Zune68 drawer.
Alternatively, change to that drawer in a Shell and run:

  SYS:System/Installer Install APPNAME Zune68 MINUSER AVERAGE

The installer installs every component below and saves each file that
Zune68 did not install first, so Set back can restore it. It asks only
when a newer version of a class that does not come with MUI, such as
NList or TextEditor, is installed; the answer can apply to all such
classes. MUI's own classes and libraries are replaced whatever their
version. The protocol lists each replaced version.

  muimaster.library   -> LIBS:muimaster.library
    Replaces MUI. The zunemaster.library of earlier betas is removed.

  Prefs/Zune         -> SYS:Prefs/Zune (with Workbench icon)
    The preferences editor used by application settings menus. Project /
    "Import MUI 3.8 settings..." reads a MUI 3.8 settings file (from
    ENVARC:MUI, or a preset from MUI:Presets) into the editor for a test;
    Save or Use keeps it. MUI 3.8's global file also brings each program's
    own MUI 3.8 settings into ENVARC:zune. When Zune has no settings yet and
    ENVARC:MUI has MUI 3.8's, the editor offers the import when it starts.
    MUI's files are only read. The System page's "MUI Version" sets the
    version programs see: MUI 5, or MUI 3.8 for programs and classes that
    lay out better with it, for all programs or by a program's file name.
    A program that asks for MUI 4 or later always sees MUI 5. Use and Save
    write ENV:zune/MUIVersion, which applies to programs started after.

  Libs/MUI/*         -> MUI:Libs/MUI/
    BetterString, HotkeyString, NBitmap, NBalance, NList, NListview,
    NFloattext, NListtree, TextEditor, TheBar, TheBarVirt, TheButton,
    the Toolbar wrapper, and their bundled MCP editors. Rawimage and
    Pixmap supply image rendering for the built-in preferences pages.
    Aboutbox, AboutWindow, Busy, Calendar, Calltips, Clock, Flexgroup,
    Graph, IconImage, Lamp, Listtree, LoginWindow, PrefsEditor, PrefsWindow,
    QRCode and SystemPrefsWindow are the AROS utility classes. Listtree.mcp
    is Listtree's page in Zune prefs, with MUI 3.8's settings. Textinput and
    Textinputscroll 29 are names for String, which has the Textinput API.
    These plugins open the standard muimaster.library name.
    Plugins are loaded on demand, not added to every application's cost.

  Locale/Catalogs/*  -> LOCALE:Catalogs/<system-language>/
    The installer maps package language names to Amiga names, for example german to deutsch.
    Incomplete or unconvertible translations fall back to English.

MUI classes belong in MUI:Libs/MUI, where MUI applications and the
installers of other MUI classes expect them. If MUI: is not assigned,
the installer asks for a drawer (SYS:MUI by default), assigns MUI: to
it, adds MUI:Libs to LIBS:, and puts the same two commands in a Zune68
section of S:User-Startup, as MUI's own installer does. With MUI:
already assigned, nothing in S:User-Startup changes.

A class in LIBS:MUI is found before the one in MUI:Libs/MUI, so an
older copy there would hide the installed class, so installing renames
it to <class-name>.zune68-old. Earlier Zune68 betas installed their classes to
LIBS:MUI; copies of the bundled version there are deleted instead.

Before replacing a file, the installer saves the original beside it as
<component-name>.zune68-old. An existing backup is preserved. A newer
resident library can remain active until reboot; the installer reports
the resident version separately and does not expunge libraries in use.
Catalogs and the Prefs icon have short .old backups. Saved preferences
are not replaced. Tests/opentest, Examples, SDK, Docs, and Source.tar.gz
remain in the extracted package. No SDK assigns are changed.

Protocol and Set back
---------------------
Each run appends what it did to MUI:Zune68/Install.log: every file it
installed, saved, renamed or removed, and the MUI: setup. Markers in
MUI:Zune68/Created and MUI:Zune68/Replaced record which files Zune68
added and which originals it saved.

When Zune68 is already installed, the installer first asks whether to
install or update, or to set back. Set back puts every saved original
back in place, deletes the files Zune68 added, and, if Zune68 set up
MUI:, removes the assign and disables the Zune68 section in
S:User-Startup. Files without a record, such as those that earlier
betas installed where nothing was before, are kept and listed in the
protocol. Close applications and reboot afterwards.

If MUI applications no longer start and Workbench is not usable, boot
without startup-sequence and restore the main library in a Shell:

  Copy SYS:Libs/muimaster.library.zune68-old SYS:Libs/muimaster.library

Without that backup there was no muimaster.library before Zune68;
delete SYS:Libs/muimaster.library or install MUI again. Check that a
backup is the MUI version you want before copying it back.
Pretend mode shows the run without copying files.

After installation, close applications and reboot before trying Zune68.
Run Tests/opentest from the extracted drawer.

The native executables request at least 65536 bytes of stack and free
any replacement stack on exit.

Normal builds omit Zune drawing/input traces. The test still prints its
own progress and results. Source builds can enable tracing with TRACE=1;
that output is deliberately excluded from performance measurements.

Developing with the SDK
-----------------------
Add SDK/include to the include path and link with -lmui, which the
m68k-amigaos GCC toolchains supply, for example in SDK/examples:

  m68k-amigaos-gcc -noixemul -Os -I../include -o HelloZune HelloZune.c -lmui

MUI_NewObject, MUI_MakeObject, MUI_Request, MUI_AllocAslRequestTags and
MUI_AslRequestTags are then libmui functions, so the usual
"ApplicationObject, ..., End" style compiles with any GCC version.
Defining MUIMASTER_YES_INLINE_STDARG selects inline tag-array macros
instead; they cannot be closed by End, and GCC 14 and later need an
(IPTR) cast for pointer tag values. As in MUI's SDK, CheckMark, KeyButton
and the other old shortcut macros need MUI_OBSOLETE.

Status and source
-----------------
This is the sixth beta release, dated [RELEASE]. Its muimaster.library
reports version [VERSION], the version of MUI 5; Zune68 releases are told
apart by the date in the version string, so Installer shows an upgrade
between them as a same-version replacement. IBrowse 3.1 and 3.0a, YAM
2.9, Odyssey, AmiPG and Voyager 3.5.2 run; IBrowse is pretty usable. Voyager
needs Textinput.mcc 29 or later: copy Textinput.mcc and Textinputscroll.mcc
over older ones in Voyager's Libs/mui drawer.

All 12 built-in Prefs pages display images; MCP pages appear once. Prefs
Test/Cancel and TheBarDemo work. See Compatibility for application results
and known limitations.

There are many ways Zune68 can still improve, and broad MUI application
coverage needs real-world use. We need testers. Please try your MUI
applications with Zune68 and report what works and what doesn't, with the
application version, CPU/OS configuration, reproduction steps and any
crash information.

Windows use smart refresh by default, as in MUI 3.8; MUI 3.9 and 5 switched
to simple refresh. Smart refresh keeps covered window contents, so
applications that draw outside MUI's redraw (AmiPG's keyboard) stay intact,
and uncovered windows come back faster. It needs memory for covered areas;
choose Simple under Refresh on the Windows page to save it.

The preferences editor includes the upstream PrefsWindow implementation
and uses Rawimage/Pixmap for its built-in page icons. muiscreen.library
is not built; public-screen enumeration has a native fallback. BetterString uses ordinary text rendering where the
classic CyberGraphX SDK lacks alpha-text support; NBitmap uses its
existing software alpha fallback. Imported plugins retain compiler
warnings, and translations are partial.

